sd2snes_gbc -- Game Boy Color core
==================================

Licence
-------
GPL-2.0-only, like the rest of the sd2snes FPGA sources.


Origin
------
This core is derived from the Super Game Boy core of the sd2snes project
(GPLv2), which implements the SGB2 cartridge: the SM83 CPU, PPU, APU, timers,
serial port and the cartridge mappers.  Those files are carried over here
essentially unchanged.

What is new in this core is the SNES-facing half.  The SGB's ICD2 chip
(sgb_icd2.v), which packed the Game Boy's 2-bit pixel output into scanline
buffers for the SNES boot ROM to fetch, is replaced by:

  gbc_clk.v     Game Boy clock-enable generation
  gbc_bridge.v  the SNES bridge: read-only views over the Game Boy's own state
                in cartridge banks $E0-$E3, and byte-wide commands in $EF00xx

The picture is re-rendered on the SNES side from those views instead of being
composed in the FPGA.  The protocol between the two halves is specified in
GBC-CORE-CONTRACT.md.

  c6_fb.v       (wire $03) the optional framebuffer mode: the PPU's own CGB
                pixel, quantised to SNES direct colour, kept in block RAM and
                read by the SNES as 8bpp tiles in bank $E4 -- for the scenes
                whose per-line palette traffic the view/log path cannot carry.

  c6_sram.v     (Mk.II, wire $05) stores the same quantised 160x144 pixels in
                external SRAM at $70000-$78fff. A 2 KiB planar row cache
                serves native or 5:8 stretched SNES DMA reads. The player
                writes row 0..17 to $EF0008, waits for status +$92 to equal
                that row, then uses the existing $E4 view and ROW_DONE.
                Status +$93 bit 0 reports a sticky producer FIFO overflow;
                overflow invalidates the cache rather than exposing torn data.

The GBC configuration contains no SA-1 instance. Only Mk.II removes the
MSU-1 data/register interface, PCM buffer/interpolator, mixer and MCU controls.
The mk3/ sources preserve the original Mk.III core, including its MSU-1 path.
The hosted board-isolation check proves active source identity on both boards.
On Mk.II the CIC stages share arithmetic by rotating their state between
the existing stage clocks. They retain all 28 bits and publish the same
sample on the same clock. The audio test compares the full filter state,
volume settings and I2S pins against the original SGB implementation, with
jitter and resets. The clock test also checks the CE1 scheduling bound.
Mk.II APU period shifters use spare multiplier blocks; exhaustive hosted
checks cover every sweep-frequency/control and noise-period input.
Mk.II wave audio uses dual-port LUT RAM with per-byte validity. CPU reset
restores the same sixteen SGB2 bytes immediately; both asynchronous readers,
the existing write gate and read-before-write sample behavior are preserved.

Mk.II status
------------
The fitted GB/GBC core is now the default for all Mk.II Game Boy images,
including existing configurations. The package replaces fpga_sgb.bit and
fpga_sgb_msu.bit with fpga_gbc.bit and includes its wire-$05 SNES player and
accepted SameBoy boot ROM/license. No opt-in MCU flag is required.
The user accepted the measured -0.832 ns setup miss on 2026-11-14. The image
and Mk.II packages are marked timing-unverified; console stability has not
been established. The selected profile is gate-lut4-delay-satlut, seed 11,
normal MAP/PAR effort, with 7161 LUTs and 3584 slices in run 43612271241.
Run 43617971625 reproduced this result and generated the accepted experimental
bitstream (SHA-256 779dcd9c08076b8b9db3704e6c2626509d22bfb966e5ab06018ef6955481330b).
The Mk.II GBC port keeps both VRAM banks,
the colour pipeline, HDMA and double speed. Small memories use LUT RAM;
the optional MCU state-debug window is omitted there.
Wire $05 also omits status fields unused by the SNES player: +$0A/+$0B,
+$0D, +$59, +$5E, and the +$30..+$3F debug mirror read zero, except +$3C bit 0
retains a sticky bus-starvation fault for board validation. The count itself
is omitted on Mk.II; any fault still remains visible until CPU reset.
DIRTY_MISC.REGS is zero:
the player applies register snapshots unconditionally. The functional SEQ,
LOG_OVF, row-freeze, dirty, palette and cache-ready/overflow fields remain.
COLW_N saturates at 256 on Mk.II: the player tests only >=96 and >=256,
so additional count precision cannot change its rendering-mode policy.
The C6 dirty bitmap preserves all 360 cells. Wire $05 places row r at
status +$A0+4*r, with its original even/odd nibble alignment in the first
three bytes and a zero padding byte. The SNES still reads three bytes per
row with the same instructions and masks. The retired +$60..+$8C view reads
zero. This removes packed-byte row decoding without coarsening dirty cells
or adding DMA traffic; the wire-$03 Mk.III bitmap remains packed.
GETSYSCLK returns the unknown marker ($ffffffff) in the Mk.II GBC core.
Only the System Information menu reads it; that menu runs on FPGA_BASE,
whose frequency measurement remains available.
The player image must remain below SRAM $70000, reserved for video storage.

Build and verify on GitHub Actions (Build firmware, FPGA mode):
  fpga_cores = mk2:gbc,mk3:gbc
  ignore_timing = false
The Mk.II Actions job synthesizes, proves the selected LUT remap, places and
routes it, and generates the bitstream with the accepted setup-timing policy.
The board-isolation and functional checks run before this path. Mk.III builds
its unchanged Quartus project. Wire $05 uses gbc_snes_mk2.bin; wire $03 keeps
gbc_snes.bin. A mismatched pair fails the existing version check. No additional
image quantisation is used. gbc_mcu_check builds the default Mk.II firmware
and its matching modules against a freshly built bootstrap.
See .github/README.md for the implementation provenance and manual remap inputs.



Block RAM rules
---------------
The EP4CE15 has 56 M9K and this core uses 53 of them.  Any new memory follows
the two rules the framebuffer fit established (c6_fb.v header):

  * a RAM deeper than 512 words gets maximum_depth = 512 on its altsyncram,
    or the fitter builds it from 1K x 8 blocks and wastes a third of them;
  * a small array read by index (a few dozen entries) gets ramstyle = "logic",
    or each one is placed in a block of its own.


